-- =====================================================================
-- Strive Elite - the General Nutrition Guide
-- =====================================================================
-- Coach Yinka, Oct 25 2026: the sixth course, "frame it as a 'general
-- nutrition guide'", a guide not a plan. Nov 1: it is in the offer post,
-- so it goes live. A LESSON course, thirteen pages in seven parts: Start
-- here (three rules, the plate), A training day (before, after), Match
-- day (the night before, game morning, halftime and after), Hydration,
-- Tournament weekends, Habits (breakfast, snacks that travel, growing),
-- The parent's job. Rules kept: general guidance only, no calorie
-- counts, no supplements, no weight targets, a not-medical-advice line
-- in the intro and on the first and last pages, "general" in the name.
-- Written by me from common youth sports nutrition guidance; Coach
-- Yinka edits any page at /coach/drills. Seeded ON. Idempotent by slug;
-- refuses a partial.
-- =====================================================================

do $$
declare
  cid uuid;
  placed int;
begin
  if exists (select 1 from public.elite_courses where slug = 'general-nutrition-guide') then
    return;
  end if;
  insert into public.elite_courses (slug, name, subtitle, intro, kind, active, sort)
  values ('general-nutrition-guide', 'General Nutrition Guide', 'Fuel for a training day, a match day, and the hours between.', E'A guide, not a plan. General food guidance for a young player, nothing more.\nNo calorie counts, no supplements, no weight targets. Allergies, a medical condition or anything about weight is a conversation with your doctor, not a page in an app.\nAsk us anything here. We will tell you what we know and when to ask someone else.', 'lesson', true, 70)
  returning id into cid;
  insert into public.elite_course_steps (course_id, level_no, level_name, title, body, sort) values
    (cid, 1, 'Start here', 'Three rules', E'Everything in this guide comes back to three things.\n\nEAT ENOUGH\nA player who trains four times a week and plays on the weekend needs more food than a kid who does not. Hungry is normal. Eat.\n\nEAT ON TIME\nThe same food at the wrong time is a heavy stomach at kickoff or an empty tank at the hour mark. The pages here are mostly about when.\n\nDRINK BEFORE YOU ARE THIRSTY\nBy the time you feel thirsty you are already behind. Water through the day, a bottle at every session.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThis is general guidance for healthy young players. It is not medical advice. Allergies, a condition, or any question about weight go to your doctor first.\n\nNothing here needs a supplement, a powder or a special product. Food from the grocery store does the job.', 10),
    (cid, 1, 'Start here', 'The plate', E'The simplest picture of a good meal, and it works for breakfast, lunch and dinner.\n\nHALF THE PLATE\nFruit and vegetables. Any kind, any color, cooked or raw. More color, more good.\n\nA QUARTER\nCarbs. Rice, pasta, potatoes, bread, oats, tortillas. This is the fuel for the session; players who cut carbs run out of gas.\n\nA QUARTER\nProtein. Chicken, fish, eggs, beef, beans, yogurt, tofu. This is what repairs the legs after the work.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nOn a training day or a match day, the carb quarter grows. That is the only change.\n\nPortions: roughly your own fist for carbs, your own palm for protein. Hands grow with the player, so the portion does too.\n\nWater with the meal. Milk is fine. Soda and energy drinks are not food.', 20),
    (cid, 2, 'A training day', 'Before training', E'Train on a full tank, never a full stomach.\n\nTWO TO THREE HOURS BEFORE\nA normal meal from the plate. Lunch before an after-school session; dinner early before an evening one.\n\nTHIRTY TO SIXTY MINUTES BEFORE\nSomething small and mostly carbs. A banana. Toast with peanut butter or jam. A granola bar. Pretzels. A small bowl of cereal.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nIf practice is straight after school, the snack in the bag matters more than anything else on this page. Pack it in the morning.\n\nNothing new and nothing heavy close to a session. Fried food, a big burger or a milkshake an hour before is a stomach ache, not fuel.\n\nWater with the snack, and the bottle goes to the session full.', 10),
    (cid, 2, 'A training day', 'After training', E'The hour after a session is when the body rebuilds. Give it something.\n\nWITHIN AN HOUR\nSomething with carbs and protein together. Chocolate milk. Yogurt and fruit. A sandwich. Cheese and crackers with an apple. A smoothie made with milk or yogurt.\n\nTHEN A REAL MEAL\nDinner from the plate when you get home, even if it is late. Skipping dinner after a hard session is the most common mistake a busy family makes.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nA late practice is not a reason to skip eating. Eat something before you go, eat a real meal after, go to bed.\n\nSore the next day is normal. Sore every day for a week usually means not enough food or not enough sleep, not too much training.', 20),
    (cid, 3, 'Match day', 'The night before', E'Match day starts at dinner the night before.\n\nDINNER\nA normal plate with a bigger carb quarter. Pasta, rice or potatoes with protein and vegetables. Nothing new, nothing spicy you have not had before, nothing greasy.\n\nWATER\nA glass with dinner and one before bed. Starting the day hydrated is half the job.\n\nSLEEP\nNo page about food replaces sleep. Nine to ten hours for a 12 to 15 year old is normal, not a luxury.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThe night before a game is not the night to try a new restaurant. Eat what you know sits well.', 10),
    (cid, 3, 'Match day', 'Game morning', E'Eat early enough that the stomach is settled by kickoff.\n\nTHREE HOURS BEFORE, IF YOU CAN\nA proper breakfast. Oatmeal with fruit. Eggs with toast. Pancakes with fruit. Cereal with milk and a banana.\n\nAN HOUR BEFORE\nIf breakfast was early, a small carb snack. A banana, a granola bar, toast with jam.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAn eight o''clock kickoff is the hard one. Eat something at six even if you are not hungry; a banana and toast beats nothing.\n\nWater from the moment you wake up. Sips, not a liter in one go.\n\nNervous stomach is real. Keep the pre-game meal plain and familiar, and a little smaller than a normal breakfast.', 20),
    (cid, 3, 'Match day', 'Halftime and after', E'HALFTIME\nWater first. Orange slices or a few grapes are fine. Nothing heavy. A sports drink only if it is hot and you have been sweating a lot.\n\nRIGHT AFTER\nThe same recovery snack as after training, within the hour. Chocolate milk, a sandwich, yogurt and fruit.\n\nTHEN\nA real meal. After a game the body wants carbs and protein, so a big plate is right.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nLost a lot of sweat? Salt matters too. A normal salted meal takes care of it; no tablets, no special drinks needed.\n\nTwo games in one day has its own page under Tournament weekends.', 30),
    (cid, 4, 'Hydration', 'Drink before you are thirsty', E'WATER, ALL DAY\nA bottle at school, a bottle at the session. Sips through the day beat a liter at once.\n\nTHE CHECK\nPale yellow is hydrated. Dark yellow means drink more today. Simple and true.\n\nSPORTS DRINKS\nFor a long, hot session or a tournament day, a sports drink can replace what sweat takes out. For a normal practice, water is all you need.\n\nNEVER\nEnergy drinks. Caffeine and a young heart at a soccer game is a bad mix, and the sugar crash lands in the second half.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nHot day: start drinking the day before, not at the field.\n\nHeadache after training is usually water, not training. Drink and eat before you worry.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'Tournament weekends', 'Two or three games in a day', E'BREAKFAST\nEarly and proper, as on any game morning.\n\nBETWEEN GAMES\nSmall, familiar, mostly carbs. Sandwiches, fruit, pretzels, crackers, a granola bar. Eat within the first hour after the game so it has time to settle before the next one.\n\nNOT BETWEEN GAMES\nThe concession stand. Fried food, hot dogs, candy and soda between games is the classic second-game crash.\n\nDRINK\nWater all day, a sports drink if it is hot and the games stack up.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nPack the cooler at home. Whatever is in the cooler is what gets eaten; whatever is not is bought at the stand.\n\nShade between games. Out of the sun, feet up, eat, drink.\n\nDinner that night is a big plate, and the next morning starts again at breakfast.', 10),
    (cid, 6, 'Habits', 'Breakfast every day', E'The one meal that gets skipped and should never be.\n\nWHY\nA player who skips breakfast is running the school day on empty and arriving at practice already behind. Energy, focus in class, mood, and the quality of the session all start here.\n\nWHAT\nAnything from the plate. Oatmeal, eggs, toast, cereal with milk, yogurt with fruit, a smoothie. Leftovers from dinner count.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNot hungry in the morning? Start small and build. A banana and a glass of milk is a breakfast.\n\nTen minutes. Set the alarm ten minutes earlier if that is what it takes.', 10),
    (cid, 6, 'Habits', 'Snacks that travel', E'What goes in the bag so there is always something to eat before a session.\n\nTHE LIST\nBananas, apples, oranges. Granola bars. Pretzels. Crackers and cheese. Peanut butter sandwiches. Trail mix. Yogurt in a cooler. Dried fruit. Rice cakes.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nBuy for the week on Sunday and the problem is solved for the week.\n\nA snack that is already in the bag gets eaten. A snack that needs a stop on the way does not.', 20),
    (cid, 6, 'Habits', 'Growing', E'Growth spurts change the rules for a while.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS\nA player in a growth spurt is hungrier, more tired, sometimes clumsier for a few months. That is the body building, and it needs more food and more sleep, not less.\n\nWHAT TO DO\nFeed the hunger with the plate, not with restriction. Bigger portions, an extra snack, the same good food. Protect sleep.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNever put a growing player on a diet. If a parent is worried about weight in either direction, that conversation is with the doctor, not with a page or with us.\n\nKnees and heels can hurt during a spurt. Tell us, and tell the doctor if it lasts. We adjust the week around it.', 30),
    (cid, 7, 'The parent''s job', 'What helps', E'FOOD IN THE HOUSE\nIf the snacks on the Habits page are in the kitchen, they get eaten. If they are not, something else does.\n\nTIMING\nDinner timed to practice, a snack packed in the morning, breakfast on the table. Most of good nutrition for a young player is a parent''s calendar.\n\nTHE MODEL\nPlayers eat the way the house eats. A family plate that looks like the plate on page one does more than any rule.\n\nWHAT HURTS\nComments about a player''s body, weight or size, from anyone, in either direction. Diets. Supplements and powders sold for kids. Energy drinks.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThis guide is general and it is not medical advice. Allergies, a condition, a sudden change in eating or weight, or a player who seems to be restricting food: talk to your doctor, and feel free to tell us so the training fits.\n\nQuestions about any of this go in the app chat. We will tell you what we know and when to ask someone else.', 10);
  select count(*) into placed from public.elite_course_steps where course_id = cid;
  if placed <> 13 then
    raise exception 'General Nutrition Guide seed placed % of 13 pages', placed;
  end if;
end $$;
